es place. \n\nLet me write this out step by step. \n\nFirst, I'll multiply the ones digits: 3 (from 73) and 4 (from 24). 3 times 4 is 12. So I write down 2 and carry over 1 to the next step.\n\nNext, I multiply the tens digits: 7 (from 73) and 2 (from 24). That gives 14. But don't forget the carry-over 1 from the previous step. So 14 plus 1 is 15. Then I write down 5 and carry over 1 again.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits to multiply, I just add the carry-over 1 to the result. So the final product should be 1522. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try breaking down 24 into 20 and 4. So 73 times 20 plus 73 times 4. \n\nCalculating 73 times 20 first. 73 times 20 is the same as 73 times 2 times 10. 73 times 2 is 146, so 146 times 10 is 1460. \n\nThen, 73 times 4. Let's compute that. 70 times 4 is 280, and 3 times 4 is 12, so 280 plus 12 is 292. \n\nNow, add 1460 and 292 together. 1460 + 292. Let's break it down: 1460 + 200 is 1660, then add 92 more. 1660 + 92 is 1752. Wait, that's different from the previous result of 1522. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nLet me check my first method again. Multiplying 73 and 24 using the standard algorithm:\n\nFirst step: 3*4=12, write 2, carry 1.\n\nSecond step: 7*2=14, plus the carried 1 is 15, write 5, carry 1.\n\nThird step: carry over 1.\n\nSo the result is 1522. But when I broke it down into 1460 + 292, I got 1752. These two results are different. That means I must have made a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check both again.\n\nStarting with the standard multiplication:\n\nFirst, 3*4=12. Correct. So write 2, carry 1. Then 7*2=14, plus 1 is 15. Write 5, carry 1. Then add the carried 1. So 1522. That seems right.\n\nNow, breaking down 24 into 20 and 4:\n\n73*20: 73*2=146, 146*10=1460. Correct.\n\n73*4: 70*4=280, 3*4=12, 280+12=292. Correct.\n\nAdding 1460 + 292: Let's do this again. 1460 + 2