method. Let's see. 26 \u00d7 45. If I think of 45 as 9 \u00d7 5, so 26 \u00d7 9 \u00d7 5. \n\n26 \u00d7 9. Let's compute that. 20 \u00d7 9 = 180, 6 \u00d7 9 = 54. So 180 + 54 = 234. Then multiply by 5: 234 \u00d7 5. 200 \u00d7 5 = 1000, 34 \u00d7 5 = 170. So 1000 + 170 = 1170. \n\nSame result again. Okay, so all methods are leading to 1170. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use the calculator-like multiplication. Let's write it out step by step:\n\nMultiply 26 by 45.\n\nStart with 26\n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a045\n--------\nFirst, multiply 26 by 5: 130\nThen multiply 26 by 40 (shifted one position left): 1040\nAdd them together: 130 + 1040 = 1170\n\nYep, same as before. \n\nI think that's thorough. All different methods confirm that the product is 1170. So I can be confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 45 is \\boxed{1170}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 45, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Method:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     26 \\\\\n   \\times 45 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     130 \\quad \\text{(26 \u00d7 5)} \\\\\n    1040 \\quad \\text{(26 \u00d7 40, shifted one position left)} \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1170 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Adding the partial products: \\(130 + 1040 = 1170\\).\n\n2. **Breaking Down 26 into 20 + 6:**\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 45 + 6 \\times 45 = 900 + 270 = 1170\n   \\]\n\n3. **Using Distributive Property:**\n   \\[\n   (25 + 1) \\times 45 = 25 \\times 45 + 1 \\times 45 = 1125 + 45 = 1170\n   \\]\n\n4. **Breaking Down 45 into 9 \u00d7 5:**\n   \\[\n   26 \\times 9 \\times 5 = 234 \\times 5 = 1170\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 26 and 45 is \\(\\boxed{1170}\\).",
