      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 67 and 65. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two two-digit numbers can be done in different ways, maybe breaking them down or using some multiplication techniques. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. So, 67 times 65. Let me write it out like how I would on paper. \n\nFirst, I'll write 67 multiplied by 65. So, 67  \n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a065  \n------  \nNow, I need to multiply 67 by 5 first, because 5 is the ones place. 67 times 5. Let me calculate that. 60 times 5 is 300, and 7 times 5 is 35. So, 300 + 35 is 335. So, that's the first part. \n\nThen, I need to multiply 67 by the tens place, which is 6. But since it's the tens place, I have to remember to shift the result one position to the left. So, 67 times 6. Let's do that. 60 times 6 is 360, and 7 times 6 is 42. Adding those together gives 360 + 42 = 402. Then, shift it one place left, so it becomes 4020. \n\nNow, add the two results together: 335 and 4020. Let's add them step by step. 335 + 4000 is 4335, and then add the remaining 20 to get 4355. So, the product of 67 and 65 is 4355. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe there's another way to verify. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property. Let's break down 65 into 60 + 5. So, 67 times 60 plus 67 times 5. \n\n67 times 60: 67 times 6 is 402, so times 10 is 4020. \n\n67 times 5: 67 times 5 is 335. \n\nAdding those together: 4020 + 335. Let's do that. 4020 + 300 is 4320, then +35 is 4355. Same result. Okay, that checks out. \n\nAnother way could be to use the formula (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2, but I don't think that applies here because 67 and 65 are not conjugates. Let me see. If I take a = 66 and b = 1, then (66 + 1)(66 - 1) = 66\u00b2 - 1\u00b2. But 66 squared is 4356, minus 1 is 4355. Oh, that's another way to get the same answer! So, that's a clever shortcut. \n\nSo, using that method, I also get 4355. That's reassuring. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the lattice method. Hmm, that might be more complicated, but let's try. \n\nFor 67 \u00d7 65, set up a lattice. \n\nFirst, write 67 and 65 as rows. \n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a067  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00d7\u00a0\u00a065  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0------  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a05\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a035  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a060\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0402  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0------\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0------  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0335\u00a0\u00a0\u00a04020  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0------\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0------  \n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a04355  \n\nWait, that's the same as the standard multiplication method. So, yeah, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use mental math. Let's see. 67 times 60 is 4020, and 67 times 5 is 335. Adding those gives 4355. Yep, same answer. \n\nI think all methods are leading me to 4355. Let me just verify with another check. Maybe using another multiplication technique. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the fact that 67 is 70 - 3 and 65 is 70 - 5. So, (70 - 3)(70 - 5). Let's expand that. \n\n70*70 = 4900  \n70*(-5) = -350  \n-3*70 = -210  \n-3*(-5) = 15  \nAdding all those together: 4900 - 350 - 210 + 15. \n\nFirst, 4900 - 350 is 4550. Then, 4550 - 210 is 4340. Then, 4340 + 15 is 4355. Again, same result. \n\nWow, that's a lot of different methods all leading to 4355. I think that's solid. \n\nJust to be absolutely sure, maybe I can use a calculator. But since I don't have a calculator here, I'll just do a quick check. \n\nLet me compute 67 \u00d7 65. \n\nFirst, 60 \u00d7 60 = 3600  \n60 \u00d7 5 = 300  \n7 \u00d7 60 = 420  \n7 \u00d7 5 = 35  \nWait, maybe that's not the right breakdown. Let me think. \n\nAlternatively, 67 \u00d7 65. Let's use the standard multiplication again. \n\n67  \n\u00d7\u00a0\u00a065  \n------  \n335 (67 \u00d7 5)  \n4020 (67 \u00d7 60, shifted one position)  \n------  \n4355  \n\nYes, same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the difference of squares. Wait, 67 and 65 are two numbers that are 2 apart. So, (66 + 1)(66 - 1) = 66\u00b2 - 1\u00b2. 66 squared is 4356, minus 1 is 4355. That's the same as before. \n\nSo, all methods converge to 4355. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 67 and 65 is 4355. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 65 is \\boxed{4355}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 65, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 67 by 5 (ones place):\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 5 = 335\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 67 by 6 (tens place), shifting the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 6 = 402 \\quad \\text{(shifted to the left becomes 4020)}\n   \\]\n3. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   335 + 4020 = 4355\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n1. Break down 65 into 60 and 5:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 60 + 67 \\times 5\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 60 = 4020 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 67 \\times 5 = 335\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   4020 + 335 = 4355\n   \\]\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares:\n1. Recognize that 67 and 65 are 2 apart:\n   \\[\n   (66 + 1)(66 - 1) = 66^2 - 1^2\n   \\]\n2. Calculate:\n   \\[\n   66^2 = 4356 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4356 - 1 = 4355\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 67 and 65 is \\(\\boxed{4355}\\).",
