al products together: 486 + 2430. Let's add them step by step. 486 + 2430. 400 + 2400 = 2800, 80 + 30 = 110, 6 + 0 = 6. So 2800 + 110 = 2910, plus 6 is 2916. \n\nWait, so the product is 2916? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try breaking down 81 and 36 into factors. \n\nI know that 81 is 9 times 9, and 36 is 4 times 9. So maybe there's a way to combine these. Let's see:\n\n81 × 36 = (9 × 9) × (4 × 9) = 9 × 9 × 4 × 9. \n\nMultiplying the 9s first: 9 × 9 = 81, then 81 × 4. Let's compute 81 × 4. 80 × 4 = 320, and 1 × 4 = 4, so 320 + 4 = 324. \n\nSo the product is 324. Wait, that's different from the previous answer. Hmm, so which one is correct? \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again. \n\nFirst method: 81 × 36. \n\nFirst partial product: 6 × 81 = 486. \n\nSecond partial product: 3 × 81 = 243, shifted left by one, so 2430. \n\nAdding 486 + 2430. Let's do this again. 486 + 2430. \n\n486 + 2430. Let's add 486 + 2430. \n\n486 + 2430. Break it down: 400 + 2400 = 2800, 80 + 30 = 110, 6 + 0 = 6. So 2800 + 110 = 2910, plus 6 is 2916. \n\nBut when I did the factoring method, I got 324. That's a discrepancy. Which one is right? \n\nWait, maybe I messed up the factoring method. Let me check that again. \n\n81 × 36. \n\nExpressed as (9 × 9) × (4 × 9). \n\nMultiplying the 9s first: 9 × 9 = 81. Then 81 × 4. \n\n81 × 4: 80 × 4 = 320, 1 × 4 = 4. So 320 + 4 = 324. \n\nBut 324 is different from 2916. So clearly, one of the methods is wrong. \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the factoring approach. Let me check the original problem again. \n\nWait, 81 × 36. If I factor 81 as 9 × 9 and 36 as 4 × 9, then multiplying them together is (9 × 9) × (4 × 9) = 9 × 9 × 4 × 9. \n\nBut when you multiply numbers, the order doesn't matter because multiplication is commutative and associative. So 9 × 9 × 4 × 9 can be rearranged as (9 × 9) × (4 × 9) or (9 × 4) × (9 × 9). \n\